<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for linger.
  - device.audio-input: recording a sermon from the microphone (src/recorder.rs). Without it the
    hardened runtime hands the app silence even after the user allows the microphone. The prompt's
    wording is NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in the Info.plist that scripts/bundle-macos.sh writes.
    Recording the computer's audio (a Core Audio process tap) needs no entitlement, only
    NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription there.
  It needs nothing else:
  - API keys live in the login Keychain through the legacy SecKeychain API (keyring's
    "keychain" store). That needs no keychain-access-groups entitlement and no provisioning
    profile. Each item's access list names the app's designated requirement (bundle ID and
    team), so later releases signed by the same team read the keys without a prompt.
  - Metal compiles shaders in a system service: no JIT or unsigned-memory exceptions.
  - The app is not sandboxed, so network access and files the user picks need no entitlement.
  - It loads no third-party libraries at run time, so library validation stays on.
  Add an entitlement only with a note saying which feature needs it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key><true/>
</dict>
</plist>
